demographics.narrative:
India's population was about 1.46 billion in 2025, and the UN projects a peak of about 1.7 billion in the early 2060s.{{1}}{{15}} The Sample Registration System puts the total fertility rate at 1.9 in 2024, below replacement in every state except six in the north: Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Rajasthan, Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand.{{3}}{{4}}{{5}} State rates ranged in 2024 from 2.9 in Bihar to 1.2 in Delhi.{{3}}{{5}}

India therefore has two demographic profiles. UNFPA expects the 60-plus share to pass 20 percent by 2036 in southern states such as Kerala and Tamil Nadu, and nationally to rise from 10.5 percent in 2022 to 20.8 percent by 2050.{{6}}{{7}} The northern states remain younger, with heavy maternal and child health needs, although national infant mortality fell to 24 per 1,000 live births in 2024 from 30 in 2019.{{4}}

The Census of 2027, the first since 2011, began house-listing in April 2026 and will enumerate the population in February 2027.{{14}} For a foreign academic medical center, the ageing south and west and the National Capital Region are the most natural markets for high-acuity collaboration, while the younger northern states suit public-health and capacity-building work.

economy.narrative:
The IMF's July 2026 World Economic Outlook Update describes India as among the fastest-growing major economies, with growth projected at 6.4 percent in FY2026-27.{{14}} Official provisional estimates put real GDP growth at 7.7 percent in FY2025-26, up from 7.1 percent a year earlier, and at 7.8 percent in April to June 2026.{{1}}{{2}} The World Bank puts 2025 GDP at about US$17.2 trillion in purchasing-power terms, or about US$11,748 per person.{{3}}{{4}} India is therefore a very large market in which a relatively small affluent and insured segment drives demand for premium care.

US trade policy is a major risk. The US raised tariffs on most Indian goods to 50 percent from 27 August 2025 over Russian oil purchases.{{5}} After the US Supreme Court struck down the IEEPA tariffs on 20 February 2026, most Indian goods have faced a 10 percent Section 301 duty since 24 July 2026.{{6}}{{7}} Generic pharmaceuticals remain under a Section 232 investigation, and a law signed on 18 September 2026 lets the President impose tariffs of up to 100 percent on the largest buyers of Russian energy, with India among the most exposed.{{8}}{{9}}{{10}} The United States took about US$9.5 billion of India's record US$31.1 billion in pharmaceutical exports in FY2025-26.{{11}}

The Union Budget 2026-27 gave the Health Ministry Rs 1,06,530 crore, nearly 10 percent above the previous year's revised estimate, and backed five regional medical hubs built with states and private investors.{{12}}{{13}}

disease_prevalence.narrative:
Non-communicable diseases now dominate India's disease burden.{{4}} The NCD share of deaths rose from 37.9 percent in 1990 to 61.8 percent in 2016 and was 66 percent in 2019, according to WHO.{{3}}{{1}} A 30-year-old Indian had a 23.6 percent chance in 2021 of dying before 70 from one of the four main NCDs.{{2}} Circulatory disease caused 38.8 percent of medically certified deaths in 2024, but only 23.1 percent of registered deaths were certified.{{5}}

ICMR-INDIAB, published in 2023, estimated 101 million people with diabetes and 136 million with prediabetes.{{6}} Air pollution, high blood pressure, diet, high fasting plasma glucose and tobacco are leading risk factors, and air pollution was linked to about 2.1 million deaths in 2021.{{7}}{{8}}{{9}}{{10}} ICMR expects cancer cases, about 1.53 million in 2024, to keep rising, led by oral and lung cancer in men and breast and cervical cancer in women.{{13}}{{14}}

India had about a quarter of the world's TB cases in 2024 despite a 21 percent fall in incidence since 2015, and 57 percent of women aged 15 to 49 were anaemic in 2019-21.{{15}}{{16}}{{12}} For a foreign academic partner, demand is clearest in cardiology, oncology, metabolic and transplant medicine, and pulmonary research.

public_system.narrative:
India's public system is tiered. Sub-health centres and primary health centres, many upgraded into Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, feed community health centres, district hospitals, state medical college hospitals and national institutes such as AIIMS, PGIMER Chandigarh and Tata Memorial Centre.{{1}}{{2}}{{6}} The Health Ministry reported 1,81,873 operational Ayushman Arogya Mandirs by 30 November 2025, offering 12 service packages including NCD screening and teleconsultation.{{1}}

The 2026-27 Union budget gives the National Health Mission Rs 39,390 crore and the PM-Ayushman Bharat Health Infrastructure Mission Rs 4,770 crore.{{3}} PM-JAY, a tax-funded scheme, pays for up to Rs 5 lakh of hospital care per family per year across 1,961 procedures at more than 38,000 empanelled public and private hospitals, and since September 2024 it has covered everyone aged 70 and over.{{4}}{{5}}

Capacity is the main weakness. Public facilities from primary health centres to medical colleges had 8,18,661 beds on 31 March 2023, fewer than 0.6 per 1,000 people against the National Health Policy 2017 norm of 2, and supply is far thinner in Bihar and Uttar Pradesh.{{6}}{{7}} Public care is much cheaper: average out-of-pocket spending per hospitalisation, excluding childbirth, was Rs 6,631 in public hospitals in 2025, against Rs 50,508 in private hospitals.{{8}}{{9}} For a foreign academic center, public apex institutions suit research, training and protocol work, while PM-JAY tariffs set the price floor that private partners compete against.

private_system.narrative:
Private hospitals handled 57.9 percent of rural and 64.6 percent of urban hospitalisations in 2025, according to the NSS 80th round, counting those empanelled under government schemes.{{1}}

The corporate chains are consolidating. Manipal Hospitals reported 49 hospitals and 13,037 licensed beds at 31 March 2026, up from 33 hospitals and 9,520 beds two years earlier, and listed in August 2026.{{2}}{{3}} Apollo Hospitals reported 10,970 beds in 78 hospitals in India and overseas at March 2026, including managed hospitals and day-surgery and cradle units.{{4}}{{5}} Aster DM Quality Care, formed by the July 2026 merger of Aster DM Healthcare with Blackstone-backed Quality Care India, has 39 hospitals and more than 10,600 beds.{{6}}{{7}} Max Healthcare had 4,966 operational beds at 31 March 2026 and aims to nearly double bed capacity in five years, and Medanta had about 3,665 installed beds after adding 623 in FY2025-26.{{9}}{{10}}{{8}} IHH Healthcare held about 31.17 percent of Fortis in November 2025.{{11}}

EY-Parthenon found revenue and EBITDA growth above 15 percent at leading operators in FY2025-26, occupancy mostly between 60 and 75 percent, and large bed pipelines.{{12}}{{13}} KKR agreed in August 2026 to buy Medicover India.{{14}} For a foreign academic medical center, these chains are the most likely commercial counterparties for affiliations, second-opinion services and clinical program development.

education.narrative:
The National Medical Commission, which replaced the Medical Council of India in September 2020, regulates medical education nationally.{{10}} Admission to undergraduate medical courses runs through one exam, NEET-UG, conducted by the National Testing Agency.{{1}} The agency cancelled the 3 May 2026 sitting on 12 May over alleged irregularities, including a reported question-paper leak, and the government referred the case to the Central Bureau of Investigation.{{3}}{{12}} Close to 20 lakh candidates sat the re-examination on 21 June 2026, and 11.21 lakh qualified.{{1}}{{2}}

NMC's seat matrix for 2026-27 lists 1,40,664 MBBS seats in 839 colleges, 64,121 of them in government colleges, against 51,348 seats in 2014.{{4}}{{5}}

Postgraduate training is the bottleneck. The government reported 82,059 postgraduate seats in December 2025, up from 31,185 in 2014, while NMC counted about 57,500 MD and MS seats for 2025-26.{{5}}{{6}} The National Board of Examinations in Medical Sciences, an autonomous body under the Health Ministry, adds DNB and DrNB training in accredited institutions.{{11}} In October 2025 the NMC chair indicated that the National Exit Test would not be implemented immediately, and a doctors' federation said mock tests would run for the next three to four years.{{7}} Mayo Clinic's advisory role for Adani Health City's two planned medical colleges and the education pillar of Cleveland Clinic's Navi Mumbai MediCity show US institutions using graduate medical education as a way into India.{{8}}{{9}}

outlook.narrative:
India's health policy has a clear direction: primary care through Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, hospital financing through a now-national PM-JAY, and a digital layer. By 10 July 2026 the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission had created about 93.95 crore ABHA health IDs and linked more than 105 crore health records.{{1}}{{2}} This consent-based layer could support remote second opinions, tele-tumour boards and cross-border coordination.

Private hospitals are expanding with public-market capital, as in Manipal's 2026 IPO, and global private equity, as with Blackstone in Aster DM Quality Care and KKR's pending purchase of Medicover India.{{10}}{{11}}{{8}} Maharashtra's Navi Mumbai MediCity, anchored by Cleveland Clinic under a June 2026 MoU, and Adani's two Health Cities, advised by Mayo Clinic, are the leading examples of integrated health cities.{{3}}{{4}}

Medical value travel is the main uncertainty. Medical arrivals were 6.44 lakh in 2024, below 6.97 lakh in 2019, and Bangladesh, which supplied about 4.8 lakh of them, was hit by visa restrictions after August 2024.{{5}}{{6}}{{7}} Other risks include government health spending of only 1.48 percent of GDP in 2022-23, workforce shortfalls, the 2026 NEET cancellation, and possible US tariffs of up to 100 percent on buyers of Russian oil, authorised in September 2026.{{12}}{{13}}{{9}} The outlook therefore favours foreign academic centers that bring training, protocols, quality systems and research to Indian partners rather than operating hospitals directly.
